1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,351
An evaluation team asks one question many
times a day: which commits changed the

2
00:00:09,391 --> 00:00:13,507
scoring rubric? A commit is one
saved snapshot of the whole project.

3
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:18,581
The command is git log with a path. On their
repository, with its long history, the

4
00:00:18,621 --> 00:00:20,904
answer takes twenty seconds each time.

5
00:00:21,244 --> 00:00:26,228
The repository isn't broken. To answer,
Git compares the tree of every commit with

6
00:00:26,268 --> 00:00:31,312
its parent's tree, one commit after another,
to find the few that touched the path.

7
00:00:31,352 --> 00:00:36,152
A tree is a commit's listing of one
directory. The fix isn't a smaller history.

8
00:00:36,192 --> 00:00:40,930
It's a file that lets Git dismiss most
commits without looking at their trees.

9
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:43,993
Keep those twenty seconds
in mind. They come back.

10
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:49,789
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:49,829 --> 00:00:54,245
In video 104 you saw a table: slow
command, what it touches, remedy.

12
00:00:54,585 --> 00:00:59,492
Today takes four remedies from that table.
The commit-graph, for history walks and

13
00:00:59,532 --> 00:01:04,199
path-limited logs. The multi-pack-index,
for object lookups across many packs.

14
00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:07,397
Reachability bitmaps, for
serving clones and fetches.

15
00:01:07,437 --> 00:01:12,344
And two accelerators for the working tree:
the untracked cache and the file-system

16
00:01:12,384 --> 00:01:12,827
monitor.

17
00:01:13,167 --> 00:01:18,086
All four are derived data, which means
Git can rebuild them from something else.

18
00:01:18,126 --> 00:01:21,682
Each can be deleted and rebuilt.
None changes a commit ID.

19
00:01:22,022 --> 00:01:26,955
Two things about the demos. They show counts
of work, not seconds, because timings

20
00:01:26,995 --> 00:01:31,443
aren't reproducible and counters are.
And this course starts no daemon, no

21
00:01:31,483 --> 00:01:36,416
background program: the file-system monitor
is described, and the transcript shows

22
00:01:36,456 --> 00:01:38,479
only that it's off and how to ask.

23
00:01:38,819 --> 00:01:43,698
Labels: git commit-graph write, git
commit-graph verify and git multi-pack-index

24
00:01:43,738 --> 00:01:46,219
write are 🟢 SAFE. They add derived files.

25
00:01:48,059 --> 00:01:49,471
After this video you can:

26
00:01:49,811 --> 00:01:54,366
Say which two kinds of data a commit-graph
file holds and which commands each helps.

27
00:01:54,706 --> 00:01:57,814
Write a commit-graph and observe
the effect on a history query.

28
00:01:58,154 --> 00:02:01,908
Explain what a stale commit-graph
does and when Git ignores the file.

29
00:02:02,248 --> 00:02:07,001
Say what a multi-pack-index and a bitmap
are for and why bitmaps are mainly a server

30
00:02:07,041 --> 00:02:07,457
feature.

31
00:02:07,797 --> 00:02:12,130
Name the two features that speed up
git status in a large working tree.

32
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:18,364
The commit-graph. In one sentence: it is
a file that stores, for every commit, its

33
00:02:18,404 --> 00:02:22,690
parents, its root tree, its date and its
distance from the roots of history, and

34
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:27,016
optionally a compact summary of the paths
it changed, so that history walks stop

35
00:02:27,056 --> 00:02:30,963
decompressing commit objects and stop
comparing trees that didn't change.

36
00:02:31,303 --> 00:02:33,981
Two things are stored, and
they help different commands.

37
00:02:34,321 --> 00:02:38,991
First, the graph with generation numbers.
A commit's generation is larger than that

38
00:02:39,031 --> 00:02:40,012
of every ancestor.

39
00:02:40,352 --> 00:02:45,653
A walk that asks "is A an ancestor of B"
can stop as soon as it reaches commits

40
00:02:45,693 --> 00:02:49,844
whose generation is below A's,
instead of walking to the root.

41
00:02:49,884 --> 00:02:55,387
This is what git merge-base, git branch
--contains, git tag --contains and git log

42
00:02:55,427 --> 00:02:56,604
--graph gain from.

43
00:02:56,944 --> 00:03:00,998
Second, changed-path Bloom filters,
written with --changed-paths.

44
00:03:01,038 --> 00:03:06,164
For each commit, a small probabilistic set
of the paths that differ from its first

45
00:03:06,204 --> 00:03:11,456
parent. Asked "did this commit touch
services/ranker?", a filter answers "definitely

46
00:03:11,496 --> 00:03:15,740
not" or "maybe". Only the "maybe"
commits need their trees compared.

47
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:19,331
This is what git log with a
path and git blame gain from.

48
00:03:19,671 --> 00:03:24,508
Who writes the file? You can, with
git commit-graph write --reachable

49
00:03:24,548 --> 00:03:30,375
--changed-paths. git gc writes one, and the
setting is gc.writeCommitGraph, default

50
00:03:30,415 --> 00:03:34,970
true. The commit-graph maintenance
task writes one incrementally.

51
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:40,696
And fetch.writeCommitGraph does it after
fetches. Filters are written only if you

52
00:03:40,736 --> 00:03:46,563
requested them once, or with
commitGraph.changedPaths=true, which needs Git 2.52 or

53
00:03:46,603 --> 00:03:46,987
later.

54
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:52,289
Failure modes. A stale file is slower,
not wrong: Git notices commits that are

55
00:03:52,329 --> 00:03:55,816
missing from the file and
reads those from the objects.

56
00:03:55,856 --> 00:03:59,663
A damaged file is another matter,
and Lab 16.4 produces one.

57
00:03:59,703 --> 00:04:03,575
And Git stops using the file
altogether in some repositories.

58
00:04:03,615 --> 00:04:08,769
The manual's caveat: "The existence of
replace objects or commit grafts turns off

59
00:04:08,809 --> 00:04:13,899
reading or writing to the commit-graph."
The same holds in a shallow repository.

60
00:04:14,239 --> 00:04:19,331
The multi-pack-index. In one sentence:
it's one sorted index over the objects of

61
00:04:19,371 --> 00:04:24,719
many packs, so that finding an object costs
one lookup however many packs there are.

62
00:04:24,759 --> 00:04:29,594
A pack is one file that stores many
objects, and each pack has its own .idx.

63
00:04:29,934 --> 00:04:35,199
With N packs, a lookup may search N
indexes. Geometric repacking, fetches and

64
00:04:35,239 --> 00:04:40,642
partial clones all produce many packs on
purpose, so the cost has to be removed

65
00:04:40,682 --> 00:04:46,429
elsewhere: the file objects/pack/multi-pack-index
maps every object ID to a pack and

66
00:04:46,469 --> 00:04:51,252
an offset. Git reads it when
core.multiPackIndex is true, the default.

67
00:04:51,592 --> 00:04:56,409
Reachability bitmaps. In one sentence: a
bitmap file stores, for selected commits,

68
00:04:56,449 --> 00:05:01,089
one bit per object in the pack saying
whether that commit reaches it, so that a

69
00:05:01,129 --> 00:05:05,946
server can answer "which objects does this
client need" without walking the graph.

70
00:05:05,986 --> 00:05:10,863
Serving a clone or fetch starts with
enumerating objects: everything reachable from

71
00:05:10,903 --> 00:05:15,306
the wanted commits minus everything
reachable from the ones the client has.

72
00:05:15,346 --> 00:05:20,104
With bitmaps that is a few bitwise operations,
and stretches of the existing pack

73
00:05:20,144 --> 00:05:22,296
can be copied to the client verbatim.

74
00:05:22,636 --> 00:05:27,440
Why is this a server feature? The setting
repack.writeBitmaps "defaults to true on

75
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:32,224
bare repos, false otherwise". A bare
repository has no working tree, and it's the

76
00:05:32,264 --> 00:05:36,890
kind a server holds. And scalar sets
pack.useBitmaps=false on clients, with the

77
00:05:36,930 --> 00:05:41,438
reason that bitmaps "are optimized for
server-side use, not client-side use".

78
00:05:41,778 --> 00:05:46,339
The working tree. In one sentence: the
untracked cache remembers which directories

79
00:05:46,379 --> 00:05:50,996
contained no untracked files, the file-system
monitor tells Git which paths changed

80
00:05:51,036 --> 00:05:55,653
since the last command, and together they
replace the scan of the working tree by a

81
00:05:55,693 --> 00:05:56,158
question.

82
00:05:56,498 --> 00:06:01,277
Recall the two kinds of work in git status:
an lstat call per tracked file, and a

83
00:06:01,317 --> 00:06:04,430
walk of every directory in
search of untracked files.

84
00:06:04,470 --> 00:06:09,367
The untracked cache, core.untrackedCache,
attacks the second: the index records the

85
00:06:09,407 --> 00:06:14,364
modification time of each directory, and a
directory whose time hasn't changed isn't

86
00:06:14,404 --> 00:06:15,018
read again.

87
00:06:15,358 --> 00:06:20,319
Its default is keep, and feature.manyFiles=true
and scalar switch it on.

88
00:06:20,359 --> 00:06:25,737
The file-system monitor, core.fsmonitor=true,
attacks the first: a daemon, git

89
00:06:25,777 --> 00:06:31,572
fsmonitor--daemon, subscribes to the operating
system's change notifications for one

90
00:06:31,612 --> 00:06:36,434
working tree, and git status calls
lstat only on the paths it reports.

91
00:06:36,474 --> 00:06:41,574
The manual lists caveats: it doesn't
know about submodules, and it refuses

92
00:06:41,614 --> 00:06:44,353
network-mounted repositories by default.

93
00:06:44,693 --> 00:06:50,583
One fact here the textbook marks unverified,
and so do I: the first release with a

94
00:06:50,623 --> 00:06:56,296
working built-in daemon. The manual page
exists at the 2.36.0 tag, and a GitHub

95
00:06:56,336 --> 00:07:02,227
engineering post dates the feature to Git
2.37. Git 2.55 added the daemon on Linux

96
00:07:02,267 --> 00:07:08,157
according to its release notes, while the
configuration manual of 2.55 still names

97
00:07:08,197 --> 00:07:09,821
only Windows and macOS.

98
00:07:10,161 --> 00:07:14,880
When should you not use them? Both
working-tree features belong to developer

99
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:20,016
machines. A CI job that runs git status
once gains nothing from a daemon that must

100
00:07:20,056 --> 00:07:21,080
first be started.

101
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:27,498
The textbook's analogy for the commit-graph:
a railway timetable printed from the

102
00:07:27,538 --> 00:07:32,116
day's train records. Asking the timetable
is faster than reading the records, and

103
00:07:32,156 --> 00:07:35,480
it's wrong the moment the records
change without a reprint.

104
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:40,632
The analogy breaks in Git's favour. Git
notices commits that are missing from the

105
00:07:40,672 --> 00:07:45,664
file and reads those from the objects. So
a stale commit-graph is slower, not wrong.

106
00:07:46,004 --> 00:07:50,937
Extend the model to all four features of
this video: each is an index printed from

107
00:07:50,977 --> 00:07:55,061
primary data. The primary data are
the objects and the working tree.

108
00:07:55,101 --> 00:07:58,821
If an index is missing or old,
Git does the work the long way.

109
00:07:58,861 --> 00:08:03,249
That's why deleting any of them is
safe and why none of them is a backup.

110
00:08:05,089 --> 00:08:10,003
On the left, no file. For each of the 94
commits on main, Git opens the commit

111
00:08:10,043 --> 00:08:14,005
object, opens its tree, and
compares it with the parent's tree.

112
00:08:14,045 --> 00:08:18,515
Ninety-four comparisons to find the
commits that changed one directory.

113
00:08:18,855 --> 00:08:22,964
On the right, one file. For each
commit, Git consults a filter.

114
00:08:23,004 --> 00:08:26,915
"Definitely not": skip, no tree
is opened. "Maybe": compare.

115
00:08:26,955 --> 00:08:31,855
The numbers at the bottom are the ones
you will see Git report in a moment.

116
00:08:36,195 --> 00:08:41,571
No commit-graph yet. Ninety-four commits,
thirteen of which changed services/ranker.

117
00:08:41,611 --> 00:08:45,761
Git reports statistics about
changed-path filters through trace2.

118
00:08:45,801 --> 00:08:47,696
Without a file there are none.

119
00:08:48,036 --> 00:08:52,698
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a
repository in the lab shell: run ls

120
00:08:52,738 --> 00:08:56,432
.git/objects/info. Is there
a file named commit-graph?

121
00:08:56,472 --> 00:08:57,124
I'll wait.

122
00:09:01,964 --> 00:09:06,703
In a fresh lab repository that directory
is empty: there is no commit-graph file

123
00:09:06,743 --> 00:09:11,661
yet. And every history query works anyway.
That's what derived data means: Git does

124
00:09:11,701 --> 00:09:14,708
the work the long way when
the shortcut is missing.

125
00:09:15,048 --> 00:09:21,783
One file, objects/info/commit-graph. Its
header is the signature CGPH, and the chunk

126
00:09:21,823 --> 00:09:28,477
table names the parts. OIDF and OIDL: the
commit IDs, sorted, with a fan-out table.

127
00:09:28,517 --> 00:09:32,671
CDAT: tree, parents, generation
and date per commit.

128
00:09:32,711 --> 00:09:37,187
GDA2: generation data. BIDX
and BDAT: the Bloom filters.

129
00:09:37,527 --> 00:09:42,914
Ninety-four commits, thirteen real matches.
How many filters will say "maybe", and

130
00:09:42,954 --> 00:09:46,422
how many "definitely not"?
Say both numbers out loud.

131
00:09:46,462 --> 00:09:47,084
I'll wait.

132
00:09:51,924 --> 00:09:57,791
Thirteen "maybe" and 81 "definitely not".
All 13 were real, so false_positive is 0.

133
00:09:57,831 --> 00:10:03,556
For libs/schemas, 92 were dismissed. The
answer did not change, and the work fell

134
00:10:03,596 --> 00:10:09,534
from 94 comparisons to 13, and to 2. That
ratio, not a stopwatch, is why the feature

135
00:10:09,574 --> 00:10:14,160
matters: in a monorepo, most commits
do not touch any given path.

136
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:20,127
A quick quiz. A new commit that isn't in
the file. Does verify fail: yes or no?

137
00:10:20,167 --> 00:10:20,988
Your answer?

138
00:10:25,828 --> 00:10:29,728
It passes. A new commit
doesn't invalidate the file.

139
00:10:29,768 --> 00:10:35,258
If you said it fails, remember the
timetable: stale is slower, not wrong.

140
00:10:35,298 --> 00:10:40,789
With --split, the form maintenance
uses, a small file is added on top and

141
00:10:40,829 --> 00:10:46,774
commit-graph-chain lists the layers.
Layers are merged when the upper one grows

142
00:10:46,814 --> 00:10:49,198
large relative to the one below.

143
00:10:49,538 --> 00:10:54,759
A replacement object changes what the
history is, so Git stops trusting the file:

144
00:10:54,799 --> 00:10:59,564
zero statistics lines. Delete the
replacement, and the file is used again.

145
00:10:59,604 --> 00:11:04,954
The last command is the diagnostic to
remember: core.commitGraph=false bypasses the

146
00:11:04,994 --> 00:11:06,318
file for one command.

147
00:11:14,158 --> 00:11:19,859
A clone that has fetched three times with
fetch.unpackLimit=1, the setting scalar

148
00:11:19,899 --> 00:11:24,537
uses so that every fetch keeps its
pack. Four packs, four indexes.

149
00:11:24,877 --> 00:11:29,963
One new file. The signature MIDX, and the
last byte of the header shown says four

150
00:11:30,003 --> 00:11:34,836
packs. Nothing else changed, and nothing
you can observe through Git changed.

151
00:11:34,876 --> 00:11:38,064
Maintenance writes this
file as part of its repack.

152
00:11:38,104 --> 00:11:41,038
You saw --write-midx in
the trace in video 104.

153
00:11:41,378 --> 00:11:46,692
The same git gc on two repositories. Only
the bare one, which plays the server, got

154
00:11:46,732 --> 00:11:51,853
a .bitmap. The default of repack.writeBitmaps
depends on the kind of repository.

155
00:11:52,193 --> 00:11:56,444
A quick quiz. We serve one full clone
with the bitmap and one without.

156
00:11:56,484 --> 00:11:59,939
Will the two clones differ,
or be identical? Your answer?

157
00:12:04,779 --> 00:12:10,515
They're identical: 519 objects each. What
differs is the work on the serving side.

158
00:12:10,555 --> 00:12:16,362
With the bitmap, all 519 objects were
pack-reused: copied from the existing pack as

159
00:12:16,402 --> 00:12:22,279
one stretch, with no per-object work. Without
it, the same 519 objects were found by

160
00:12:22,319 --> 00:12:24,533
a walk and reused one at a time.

161
00:12:24,873 --> 00:12:29,726
Neither accelerator is on by default.
git fsmonitor--daemon status exits 1 and

162
00:12:29,766 --> 00:12:34,744
starts nothing. The untracked cache isn't
demonstrated with counters either: its

163
00:12:34,784 --> 00:12:39,762
effect depends on directory modification
times, and a transcript that depends on

164
00:12:39,802 --> 00:12:41,581
file ages isn't reproducible.

165
00:12:43,421 --> 00:12:45,069
Five mistakes to watch for.

166
00:12:45,409 --> 00:12:48,622
Deleting the commit-graph
because it is "out of date".

167
00:12:48,662 --> 00:12:53,320
Root cause: a stale file is a snapshot;
Git reads the missing commits from the

168
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,669
objects, so stale is slower, not wrong.

169
00:12:56,009 --> 00:13:00,006
Expecting the commit-graph to help
in a shallow clone or with grafts.

170
00:13:00,046 --> 00:13:04,745
Root cause: replace objects, grafts and
shallow repositories turn off reading the

171
00:13:04,785 --> 00:13:05,038
file.

172
00:13:05,378 --> 00:13:10,091
Enabling bitmaps on a developer clone.
Root cause: bitmaps answer the server's

173
00:13:10,131 --> 00:13:14,479
question of what a client needs; they
are optimized for server-side use.

174
00:13:14,819 --> 00:13:19,809
Counting packs as the problem. Root cause:
many packs are expected; many packs with

175
00:13:19,849 --> 00:13:23,566
no multi-pack-index are the sign
that maintenance has not run.

176
00:13:23,906 --> 00:13:29,156
Starting the file-system monitor in CI.
Root cause: a job that runs git status once

177
00:13:29,196 --> 00:13:32,662
gains nothing from a daemon
that must first be started.

178
00:13:34,502 --> 00:13:39,415
Now, out of the lab. The evaluation team
from the hook enables changed-path filters

179
00:13:39,455 --> 00:13:44,369
once, with git commit-graph write --reachable
--changed-paths, and lets maintenance

180
00:13:44,409 --> 00:13:49,322
keep the file current. The question "which
commits changed the scoring rubric" then

181
00:13:49,362 --> 00:13:53,022
compares trees only for the
commits whose filter says "maybe".

182
00:13:53,062 --> 00:13:56,244
That's the work behind those
twenty seconds, cut down.

183
00:13:56,284 --> 00:14:00,840
Microsoft published the effect on the
Linux and Windows repositories when the

184
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:05,853
feature was introduced in Git 2.18. The
numbers there are theirs, on their machines.

185
00:14:06,193 --> 00:14:10,817
For the working tree, the textbook cites
one published measurement: Canva reported

186
00:14:10,857 --> 00:14:15,482
git status going from 10 seconds to about
3 with the monitor, the untracked cache,

187
00:14:15,522 --> 00:14:18,383
scheduled maintenance and
sparse checkout together.

188
00:14:18,423 --> 00:14:22,935
That's their measurement. These two features
matter when the lstat and directory

189
00:14:22,975 --> 00:14:25,324
counters are in the hundreds of thousands.

190
00:14:27,164 --> 00:14:31,171
Your turn. Do Lab 16.4, "Write
and verify a commit-graph", in

191
00:14:31,211 --> 00:14:36,480
lab-manual/m16-maintenance.md. Before you
write the file, predict the statistics

192
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:41,921
line for the path the lab asks about: how
many "maybe", how many "definitely not".

193
00:14:41,961 --> 00:14:47,162
Before the failure scenario, predict
whether a damaged file makes Git slower or

194
00:14:47,202 --> 00:14:48,091
makes it fail.

195
00:14:48,431 --> 00:14:53,906
The challenge is Exercise 18.6,
"History without content", in

196
00:14:53,946 --> 00:14:56,709
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

197
00:14:58,549 --> 00:15:01,103
Question 84 of the CTO question bank:

198
00:15:01,443 --> 00:15:06,009
A strong answer names both kinds of data
with the question each one answers, and

199
00:15:06,049 --> 00:15:10,673
attaches commands to each. It treats "stale"
and "damaged" as two different cases

200
00:15:10,713 --> 00:15:15,279
with two different outcomes, and gives
the one-command diagnostic that takes the

201
00:15:15,319 --> 00:15:20,001
file out of the picture. It says what kind
of data the file is, and therefore what

202
00:15:20,041 --> 00:15:20,807
the repair is.

203
00:15:22,647 --> 00:15:27,802
Let's land this. Four shortcuts, all derived,
all safe to delete, and none of them a

204
00:15:27,842 --> 00:15:28,235
backup.

205
00:15:28,575 --> 00:15:30,235
You should now be able to say:

206
00:15:30,575 --> 00:15:35,122
The commit-graph holds generation numbers
for graph walks and changed-path filters

207
00:15:35,162 --> 00:15:36,521
for path-limited history.

208
00:15:36,861 --> 00:15:42,043
It is a cache: stale is slower, not wrong;
